clude (0,0), (0,1), (1,1). Live: 3 → survives\n  - (0,1): live, neighbors include (0,0), (1,0), (1,1). Live: 3 → survives\n  - (1,1): live, neighbors include (0,0), (1,0), (0,1). Live: 3 → survives\n  - Other cells: 0 neighbors → not born\n  - Gen 1: same as Gen 0. ✓\n  \n  Good, my logic is correct.\n  \n  So the visualization for Rule 2 example is wrong (or the Gen 0 cells aren't what's shown). And the text is also wrong.\n  \n  Actually wait, maybe the visualization of Gen 1 is correct (showing 2 cells) but Gen 0 should be different. Let me try:\n  \n  If Gen 1 = [(1,0), (1,1)], what Gen 0 would produce this?\n  - (1,0) survives → it had 2-3 neighbors\n  - (1,1) born → it had exactly 3 neighbors\n  - All other Gen 0 cells died\n  \n  For (1,1) to have exactly 3 neighbors in Gen 0:\n  - Of (1,1)'s 8 neighbors, exactly 3 must be alive\n  - And (1,1) itself is dead in Gen 0\n  \n  For (1,0) to survive:\n  - (1,0) had 2-3 neighbors\n  - One neighbor is (1,1) - but (1,1) is dead in Gen 0\n  - So (1,0) needs 2-3 other neighbors alive\n  \n  Possible Gen 0 cells: subset of (1,1)'s 8 neighbors ∪ (1,0)'s 8 neighbors minus (1,1) itself\n  \n  (1,1)'s 8 neighbors: (0,0), (1,0), (2,0), (0,1), (2,1), (0,2), (1,2), (2,2)\n  (1,0)'s 8 neighbors (excluding (1,1)): (0,0), (2,0), (0,1), (2,1), (0,-1), (2,-1), (1,-1), (1,1)\n  Combined (deduplicated): (0,0), (1,0), (2,0), (0,1), (2,1), (0,2), (1,2), (2,2), (0,-1), (2,-1), (1,-1)\n  \n  We need (1,1) to have exactly 3 live neighbors from these.\n  We need (1,0) to have 2-3 live neighbors (from its own 8 neighbors).\n  \n  Hmm this is getting complex. Let me think differently.\n  \n  What if Gen 0 is `[(0,0), (1,0), (2,0)]` (just the top row)?\n  - (1,0): live, neighbors: (0,0), (2,0) = 2 → survives\n  - (0,0): live, neighbors: (1,0) = 1 → dies\n  - (2,0): live, neighbors: (1,0) = 1 → dies\n  - (1,1): dead, neighbors: (0,0), (2,0) = 2 → not born\n  - Output: [(1,0)]\n  \n  What if Gen 0 is `[(0,0), (1,0), (2,0), (0,1), (1,1)]`?\n  - 