,1), (2,1)\n  - Row 2: (0,2), (1,2), (2,2)\n  \n  Each cell's 8 neighbors:\n  - Corner cells (0,0), (2,0), (0,2), (2,2): 3 neighbors (adjacent + 2 diagonal). All live. → survives (Rule 2)\n  - Edge cells (1,0), (0,1), (2,1), (1,2): 5 neighbors. All live. → dies (Rule 3, > 3 neighbors)\n  - Center cell (1,1): 8 neighbors. All live. → dies (Rule 3)\n  \n  Dead cells: none adjacent with exactly 3 live neighbors. Let me check:\n  - (1,1) is dead (was center, just died). Neighbors: (0,0), (2,0), (0,1), (2,1), (0,2), (2,2), (1,0), (1,2) = 8 → not born\n  - No other dead cells within the bounding box of these 9 cells. Dead cells outside have fewer neighbors.\n  \n  Actually wait, (1,-1), (1,3), (-1,1), (3,1) are dead cells just outside. Let me check:\n  - (1,-1): neighbors (0,-2), (1,-2), (2,-2), (0,-1), (2,-1), (0,0), (1,0), (2,0). Live: (0,0), (1,0), (2,0) = 3 → BORN!\n  - (1,3): symmetric. Live: (0,2), (1,2), (2,2) = 3 → BORN!\n  - (-1,1): neighbors (-2,0), (-2,1), (-2,2), (-1,0), (-1,2), (0,0), (0,1), (0,2). Live: (0,0), (0,1), (0,2) = 3 → BORN!\n  - (3,1): symmetric. Live: (2,0), (2,1), (2,2) = 3 → BORN!\n  \n  Hmm so 3x3 full grid → 4 corners + 4 \"outside\" cells = 8 cells.\n  \n  But wait, the 4 outside cells each have 3 neighbors but they're not adjacent to each other. Let me check if they should be born:\n  - (1,-1): 3 neighbors (the 3 cells of row 0). Born? In standard Game of Life, yes.\n  - (1,3): same. Born.\n  - (-1,1): 3 neighbors (the 3 cells of row 0 column 0). Born.\n  - (3,1): 3 neighbors (the 3 cells of row 0 column 2). Born.\n  \n  So Gen 1 = [(0,0), (2,0), (0,2), (2,2), (1,-1), (1,3), (-1,1), (3,1)] = 8 cells.\n  \n  This is getting complex. Let me use a simpler test for overpopulation. Like a 2x3 rectangle:\n  - Cells: (0,0), (1,0), (0,1), (1,1), (0,2), (1,2) - a 2x3 vertical block\n  - (0,0): neighbors (1,0), (0,1), (1,1) = 3 → survives\n  - (1,0): neighbors (0,0), (0,1), (1,1) = 3 → survives\n  - (0,1): neighbors (0,0), (1,0), (1,1), (0,2), (1,2) 