w.\n\nWait, one more consideration. Looking at the original code:\n\n```ts\nfor (const [k, count] of neighborCounts) {\n  const aliveNext = count === 3 || (count === 2 && live.has(k));\n  if (aliveNext) {\n    const [x, y] = k.split(\",\").map(Number);\n    next.push([x, y]);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis iterates over all keys in `neighborCounts`. These keys represent every cell that is a neighbor of some live cell. So this includes live cells themselves (since they are neighbors of their neighbors).\n\nWait, no. Live cells are NOT included because when we iterate `(x+dx, y+dy)` for dx,dy in [-1,1], we skip (0,0), so we never increment the count of the live cell itself. So `neighborCounts` contains only neighbor cells, not live cells themselves.\n\nHmm, but what about cells with exactly 2 neighbors that are alive? Those should survive. They are in `neighborCounts` because they are neighbors of some live cell. So they'll be in the iteration. Good.\n\nWhat about cells with 3+ neighbors that are alive but not in `neighborCounts`? That shouldn't happen because if a cell has 3+ live neighbors, then it's a neighbor of those 3+ cells, so it would be in `neighborCounts`.\n\nOK the algorithm is correct.\n\nNow, one more potential refactor: instead of iterating over `neighborCounts` keys (strings) and parsing them, we could iterate over the original cell positions and check if they're alive next generation. But that would require checking every possible cell in some range, which is less efficient.\n\nActually, what if we iterate over the keys differently? We could maintain the original Cell references during counting. But this requires a `Map<Cell, number>` which has the reference equality problem.\n\nOK let me just apply the refactor.\n\nLet me write the refactored code:\n\n```ts\nexport type Cell = [number, number];\n\nconst key = (x: number, y: number): string => `${x},${y}`;\n\nconst parseKey = (k: string): Cell => {\n  const [x, y] = k.split(\",\").map(Number);\n  return [x, y];